  "id": "Compound::DB00690 - Gene::2556",
  "verbalization": "Flurazepam, a benzodiazepine, is shown to bind to Gabrr2, Gabrg1, and Gabrd, all of which are subunits of the Gaba-a receptor. These receptors are critical for the modulation of neuronal activity, and their participation in Gaba-a receptor activity is linked to Gabra3, a key component of the Gaba-a receptor complex. The high scores in the visible paths suggest a strong and consistent interaction between Flurazepam and Gabra3 through the Gaba-a receptor pathway. The overlapping binding sites across different Gabra subunits indicate that Flurazepam may exert its effects through a common mechanism, possibly by modulating the activity of the Gaba-a receptor complex. This mechanism is supported by the presence of multiple pathways converging on Gabra3, suggesting that Flurazepam may influence GABAergic signaling in the central nervous system. The evidence is robust and aligns with established pharmacological principles, making the relationship between Flurazepam and Gabra3 a plausible and mechanistically sound connection.",
